===Etymology===
{{unk|la}}; possibly of {{bor|la|ett|-}} origin. The suffix seems similar to that of {{m|la|comes}}, {{m|la|eques}}, {{m|la|pedes}}, and {{m|la|veles}}, but the origin of the ''mīl-'' is obscure. A connection to {{m|la|mīlia||thousands}}, perhaps as "person going by the thousand(s)", is difficult to confidently motivate semantically.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=mīles|page=379}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|mīles|eccl=1}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|mīles/mīlit<3>|g=m|g2=f}}

# {{lb|la|military}} A [[soldier]].
#: {{uxi|la|'''mīles''' gregārius|a '''private''' (lit., a "common '''soldier'''")}}
# {{lb|la|Medieval Latin|}} A [[knight]].
# A [[man]] in boardgames such as [[ludus latrunculi]] and chess.

